{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/kodd/Nutri","sessionId":"d0a4426d-7a3e-4469-b77d-2093bf639a97","version":"2.1.31","gitBranch":"HEAD","agentId":"a66ebaa","slug":"purring-mixing-harp","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are helping design a universal health tracking system with a comprehensive food composition database (200+ compounds from 18 international sources).\n\nThe tracking model:\n**Item** (what you track): name, type (input|output|context), value_type, unit\n**Entry** (logged value): item_id, value, timestamp, notes\n\nYOUR TASK: Deep dive into FOOD-SPECIFIC correlations. Since we have detailed compound data, explore how the correlation engine could work at the COMPOUND level, not just food level.\n\nGenerate 20-25 detailed food-symptom correlation scenarios:\n\n1. **Surface level correlation**\n   - \"Pizza correlates with bloating\"\n   \n2. **Compound level insight**\n   - \"Actually, it's the gluten (wheat compounds) + lactose (dairy compounds)\"\n   - Show how breaking down foods into compounds reveals deeper patterns\n\n3. **Scenarios to cover:**\n   - FODMAP sensitivities (which specific compounds?)\n   - Histamine intolerance (foods high in histamine/histamine liberators)\n   - Oxalate sensitivity\n   - Salicylate sensitivity\n   - Nightshade reactions (solanine, etc.)\n   - Omega 6:3 ratio effects\n   - Specific amino acid patterns\n   - Micronutrient deficiencies showing as symptoms\n   - Anti-nutrient effects (phytates, lectins)\n   - Caffeine metabolism differences\n   - Tyramine sensitivity (aged foods)\n   - Sulfur compound reactions\n   - Artificial sweetener effects\n   - Specific fatty acid responses\n\nFor each:\n1. The foods the user ate\n2. The compounds those foods contain (from our database)\n3. The symptom pattern\n4. The compound-level correlation discovered\n5. The actionable insight (\"avoid high-histamine foods\" vs \"avoid tuna specifically\")\n\nThis shows the power of having compound-level data, not just calorie counting."},"uuid":"f0ca6b6a-7001-4f16-aa74-3ab4fb1a9f1a","timestamp":"2026-02-04T03:55:07.585Z"}
{"parentUuid":"f0ca6b6a-7001-4f16-aa74-3ab4fb1a9f1a","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/kodd/Nutri","sessionId":"d0a4426d-7a3e-4469-b77d-2093bf639a97","version":"2.1.31","gitBranch":"HEAD","agentId":"a66ebaa","slug":"purring-mixing-harp","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_01QS4jXY91D2M5Pwyg1rH55M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Deep Dive: Compound-Level Food-Symptom Correlation Engine\n\n## Overview\n\nThe power of Nutri's 200+ compound database isn't just tracking what you eat—it's **discovering WHY certain foods affect you**. While surface-level tracking sees \"pizza → bloating,\" compound-level analysis reveals \"gliadin proteins + lactose + high sodium → fluid retention + fermentation.\"\n\nThis document explores 25 detailed scenarios showing how compound-level correlation transforms vague food sensitivities into actionable biochemical insights.\n\n---\n\n## Correlation Engine Architecture\n\n```\nUser Meal Log                    Symptom Log\n      ↓                               ↓\n┌─────────────────┐          ┌─────────────────┐\n│ Food → Compounds│          │ Symptom Pattern │\n│ (merged_nutrients)         │ (timing, severity)\n└────────┬────────┘          └────────┬────────┘\n         │                            │\n         └──────────┬─────────────────┘\n                    ↓\n         ┌─────────────────────┐\n         │ Correlation Engine  │\n         │ ─────────────────── │\n         │ • Temporal analysis │\n         │ • Compound grouping │\n         │ • Dose-response     │\n         │ • Cofactor effects  │\n         └─────────┬───────────┘\n                   ↓\n         ┌─────────────────────┐\n         │ Insight Generation  │\n         │ \"High histamine +   │\n         │  low DAO cofactors\" │\n         └─────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 1: FODMAP - Fructan Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Bread and onions give me terrible gas and bloating\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Sourdough bread | 2 slices | FDC, CNF |\n| Caramelized onions | 100g | FDC, CIQUAL |\n| Garlic butter | 20g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis (from `merged_nutrients`)\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Confidence |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------------|\n| **Fructans (inulin-type)** | Wheat | 1.8g | 0.85 |\n| **Fructans** | Onion | 4.2g | 0.92 |\n| **Fructans** | Garlic | 1.1g | 0.88 |\n| **Total Fructans** | Combined | **7.1g** | — |\n| Lactose | Butter | 0.1g | 0.95 |\n| Gluten proteins | Wheat | 8.2g | 0.90 |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Bloating: 7/10 severity, onset 2-4 hours\n- Gas: 8/10 severity, onset 3-6 hours\n- Abdominal cramping: 5/10, onset 3-4 hours\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nCorrelation Analysis:\n├── Fructans total: 7.1g (threshold analysis: symptoms appear >3g)\n├── Pattern: 12/15 bloating episodes correlate with fructan >3g\n├── Gluten: Present but NOT correlating independently\n│   └── Sourdough (fermented, lower fructans) → less symptoms\n│   └── Regular bread (higher fructans) → more symptoms\n└── Conclusion: FRUCTAN sensitivity, not gluten sensitivity\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid bread\"** → **\"Limit fructan intake to <3g per meal\"**\n> \n> Safe alternatives: Sourdough (long ferment), rice, potatoes, corn\n> Hidden fructan sources: Wheat, onion, garlic, artichoke, leeks\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 2: Histamine Intolerance\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Fish and wine give me headaches and flushing\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Tuna sashimi | 150g | FDC, MEXT |\n| Aged parmesan | 40g | FDC, CIQUAL |\n| Red wine | 250ml | FDC, Phenol-Explorer |\n| Spinach salad | 100g | FDC, CNF |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Compound Type |\n|----------|-------------|--------|---------------|\n| **Histamine** | Tuna | 45mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Histamine** | Parmesan | 36mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Histamine** | Red wine | 8mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Histamine** | Spinach | 12mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Total Histamine** | — | **101mg** | — |\n| **Tyramine** | Parmesan | 28mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Sulfites** | Red wine | 150mg | DAO inhibitor |\n| Alcohol | Red wine | 32g | DAO inhibitor |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Flushing: 8/10, onset 20-45 minutes\n- Headache: 7/10, onset 1-2 hours\n- Nasal congestion: 6/10, onset 30 minutes\n- Heart palpitations: 4/10, onset 30 minutes\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nHistamine Load Analysis:\n├── Total histamine: 101mg (threshold: symptoms appear >50mg)\n├── DAO Inhibitors present:\n│   ├── Alcohol (32g) - blocks diamine oxidase\n│   └── Sulfites (150mg) - inhibits DAO activity\n├── Compounding effect: Histamine + DAO inhibitors = 3x worse\n└── Pattern: 18/20 flushing episodes = histamine >40mg + alcohol\n\nCritical insight: Fresh tuna (low histamine) + white wine → NO symptoms\n                 Aged tuna (high histamine) + red wine → SEVERE symptoms\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid fish and wine\"** → **\"Histamine intolerance with DAO inhibitor sensitivity\"**\n> \n> Strategy:\n> - Fresh fish only (avoid canned, aged, or raw sushi-grade that sat)\n> - White wine over red (lower histamine, fewer sulfites)\n> - Avoid combining histamine foods with DAO inhibitors\n> - Consider DAO enzyme supplementation before high-histamine meals\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 3: Oxalate Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"My healthy smoothies make me feel awful—brain fog and joint pain\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Spinach | 100g | FDC, CNF, CIQUAL |\n| Almond butter | 32g | FDC |\n| Cacao powder | 15g | FDC, FooDB |\n| Beet | 80g | FDC, CNF |\n| Swiss chard | 50g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Daily Limit |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-------------|\n| **Oxalate** | Spinach | 970mg | — |\n| **Oxalate** | Almond butter | 122mg | — |\n| **Oxalate** | Cacao | 623mg | — |\n| **Oxalate** | Beet | 152mg | — |\n| **Oxalate** | Swiss chard | 325mg | — |\n| **Total Oxalate** | — | **2,192mg** | 50-100mg (sensitive) |\n| Calcium | Combined | 180mg | Binds oxalate |\n| Magnesium | Combined | 220mg | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Brain fog: 7/10, onset 2-4 hours\n- Joint stiffness: 6/10, onset 4-8 hours\n- Fatigue: 7/10, persistent\n- Cloudy urine: noted\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nOxalate Load Analysis:\n├── Total oxalate: 2,192mg (22x sensitive person's limit!)\n├── Calcium:oxalate ratio: 0.08 (should be >1 for binding)\n├── Pattern: 14/14 brain fog episodes = oxalate >500mg\n├── Kidney stone history: YES (calcium oxalate type)\n└── \"Health halo\" foods are oxalate bombs:\n    └── Spinach smoothie = worst possible choice for this person\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"smoothies are bad\"** → **\"Severe oxalate overload from 'superfoods'\"**\n> \n> Swap list:\n> - Spinach → Kale or romaine (10x less oxalate)\n> - Almond butter → Sunflower seed butter\n> - Cacao → Carob powder\n> - Beets → Carrots\n> - Swiss chard → Bok choy\n> \n> Target: <100mg oxalate per day with calcium pairing\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 4: Salicylate Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Fruit and vegetables give me rashes and breathing issues\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Tomato sauce | 150g | FDC, CIQUAL |\n| Olive oil | 30ml | FDC, Phenol-Explorer |\n| Berries (mixed) | 150g | FDC, Duke's |\n| Curry spices | 5g | Duke's |\n| Honey | 20g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Category |\n|----------|-------------|--------|----------|\n| **Salicylic acid** | Tomato | 12mg | Salicylate |\n| **Salicylic acid** | Olive oil | 8mg | Salicylate |\n| **Salicylic acid** | Berries | 28mg | Salicylate |\n| **Salicylic acid** | Curry spices | 45mg | Salicylate |\n| **Salicylic acid** | Honey | 3mg | Salicylate |\n| **Total Salicylates** | — | **96mg** | — |\n| Polyphenols | Combined | 450mg | Enhancers |\n| Benzoates | — | 0mg | Related |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Urticaria (hives): 7/10, onset 2-6 hours\n- Rhinitis: 6/10, onset 1-2 hours\n- Asthma-like symptoms: 5/10, onset 2-4 hours\n- Tinnitus: 4/10, intermittent\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nSalicylate Pattern:\n├── Total salicylate: 96mg (threshold: 20mg for sensitive)\n├── Spice load: 45mg from 5g curry (concentrated source!)\n├── Pattern: 16/18 skin reactions = salicylate >30mg\n├── Aspirin history: Cannot tolerate (same pathway)\n├── Note: Reactions WORSE with polyphenol stacking\n└── Hidden sources identified: Mint, tea, almonds, citrus\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid fruits and vegetables\"** → **\"Salicylate sensitivity—choose low-sal options\"**\n> \n> Low salicylate alternatives:\n> - Berries → Pears, peeled apples, banana\n> - Tomato sauce → White sauce or butter-based\n> - Olive oil → Sunflower or canola oil  \n> - Curry → Saffron, garlic, fresh herbs (parsley, chives)\n> - Honey → Maple syrup (pure)\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 5: Nightshade (Solanine) Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Pasta dinners destroy my joints for days\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Marinara sauce | 200g | FDC |\n| Eggplant parmesan | 150g | FDC, CIQUAL |\n| Bell peppers (roasted) | 100g | FDC, CNF |\n| Chili flakes | 2g | Duke's |\n| Potato gnocchi | 200g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Notes |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-------|\n| **Solanine** | Potato | 2.8mg | Glycoalkaloid |\n| **Tomatine** | Tomato | 4.2mg | Glycoalkaloid |\n| **Solanine** | Eggplant | 1.8mg | Glycoalkaloid |\n| **Capsaicin** | Bell pepper | 0mg | Not the issue |\n| **Capsaicin** | Chili | 8mg | — |\n| **Total Glycoalkaloids** | — | **8.8mg** | — |\n| Lectins | Combined | High | Inflammatory |\n| Vitamin C | Combined | 85mg | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Joint pain (hands, knees): 8/10, onset 12-24 hours\n- Morning stiffness: 7/10, next day\n- Muscle aches: 6/10, 12-48 hours\n- GI upset: 4/10, same evening\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nGlycoalkaloid Pattern:\n├── Total glycoalkaloids: 8.8mg\n├── Cumulative effect: Symptoms worsen with consecutive days\n├── Pattern: 11/12 joint flares follow nightshade meals\n├── Interesting: Capsaicin (hot peppers) NOT correlating\n│   └── Bell peppers (no capsaicin) still trigger\n│   └── Confirms: Glycoalkaloids, not capsaicin\n├── Autoimmune marker: ANA positive (nightshades may worsen)\n└── Half-life issue: Glycoalkaloids take 5+ days to clear\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid Italian food\"** → **\"Eliminate nightshade glycoalkaloids for 30 days\"**\n> \n> Complete nightshade list:\n> - Tomatoes (all forms including ketchup, sauce)\n> - Potatoes (sweet potatoes are fine—not nightshades)\n> - Eggplant\n> - Bell peppers (all colors)\n> - Chili peppers\n> - Paprika, cayenne\n> - Goji berries\n> \n> Swap: Marinara → Pesto or alfredo; Potato → Sweet potato\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 6: Omega-6:3 Ratio Inflammation\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"My pain and fatigue are worst when I eat 'healthy' home cooking\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Chicken breast | 200g | FDC, CNF |\n| Brown rice | 200g cooked | FDC |\n| Vegetables in sunflower oil | 150g | FDC |\n| Almonds | 50g | FDC |\n| Whole wheat bread | 80g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis - Fatty Acids\n| Compound | Amount | Type |\n|----------|--------|------|\n| **Linoleic acid (18:2 n-6)** | 18.2g | Omega-6 |\n| **Alpha-linolenic (18:3 n-3)** | 0.8g | Omega-3 |\n| **Arachidonic acid (20:4 n-6)** | 0.2g | Omega-6 |\n| **EPA (20:5 n-3)** | 0g | Omega-3 |\n| **DHA (22:6 n-3)** | 0g | Omega-3 |\n| **Omega-6:3 Ratio** | **22.8:1** | Target: 4:1 or less |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Chronic fatigue: 7/10, constant\n- Joint inflammation: 6/10, bilateral\n- Skin inflammation: 5/10, acne and eczema\n- Slow wound healing: noticed\n- Brain fog: 6/10, afternoon\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nFatty Acid Analysis:\n├── Omega-6:3 ratio: 22.8:1 (optimal: 1:1 to 4:1)\n├── Sources of excess omega-6:\n│   ├── Sunflower oil: 8.2g linoleic acid\n│   ├── Almonds: 6.1g linoleic acid\n│   └── Chicken (grain-fed): 2.4g linoleic acid\n├── Omega-3 sources: Nearly zero\n├── 30-day trend: Ratio consistently >15:1\n├── Inflammatory markers correlate with ratio spikes\n└── Note: \"Healthy\" seed oils driving inflammation\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"your cooking is bad\"** → **\"Omega-6 excess from seed oils and grain-fed meat\"**\n> \n> Interventions:\n> - Replace sunflower/corn/soy oil → Olive oil, avocado oil, butter\n> - Add fatty fish 3x/week (salmon, sardines, mackerel)\n> - Choose pasture-raised eggs and grass-fed beef (better ratio)\n> - Add ground flaxseed or chia (plant omega-3)\n> - Target ratio: <4:1 within 30 days\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 7: Tyramine Sensitivity (MAO Interaction)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Certain dinners give me crushing headaches that last all night\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Aged cheddar | 60g | FDC, CNF |\n| Salami | 50g | FDC, CIQUAL |\n| Sauerkraut | 100g | FDC |\n| Sourdough bread | 80g | FDC |\n| Red wine | 200ml | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Notes |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-------|\n| **Tyramine** | Aged cheddar | 38mg | Biogenic amine |\n| **Tyramine** | Salami | 24mg | Fermented meat |\n| **Tyramine** | Sauerkraut | 18mg | Fermented |\n| **Tyramine** | Sourdough | 6mg | Fermented |\n| **Tyramine** | Red wine | 8mg | Fermented |\n| **Total Tyramine** | — | **94mg** | Threshold: 10-25mg |\n| Histamine | Combined | 45mg | Also elevated |\n| Phenylethylamine | Chocolate | 0mg | None present |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Migraine: 9/10, onset 30-60 minutes\n- Hypertensive feeling: 7/10, face pressure\n- Neck stiffness: 6/10, onset with headache\n- Nausea: 5/10, accompanies migraine\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nTyramine Pattern:\n├── Total tyramine: 94mg (4-9x threshold!)\n├── MAO inhibitor medication: YES (for depression)\n│   └── CRITICAL: MAOIs + tyramine = hypertensive crisis risk\n├── Pattern: 100% of migraines correlate with tyramine >20mg\n├── All trigger foods are AGED or FERMENTED\n├── Fresh versions of same foods: NO reaction\n│   └── Fresh mozzarella vs aged cheddar: No symptoms\n└── Medication interaction flagged for safety\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid charcuterie boards\"** → **\"Tyramine restriction required with MAOI medication\"**\n> \n> **SAFETY ALERT**: This interaction can cause dangerous blood pressure spikes\n> \n> Must avoid:\n> - Aged cheeses (cheddar, parmesan, blue cheese, brie)\n> - Cured meats (salami, pepperoni, prosciutto)\n> - Fermented foods (sauerkraut, kimchi, miso)\n> - Aged wine and beer (especially red wine, tap beer)\n> - Soy sauce, fish sauce\n> \n> Safe alternatives: Fresh cheeses (ricotta, cottage), fresh meats, fresh vegetables\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 8: Sulfur Compound Sensitivity (SIBO/Dysbiosis)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Eggs and cruciferous vegetables give me horrible gas and rotten smell\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Eggs (whole) | 3 large | FDC, CNF |\n| Broccoli | 150g | FDC, CNF |\n| Garlic | 10g | FDC, Duke's |\n| Onion | 80g | FDC |\n| Brussels sprouts | 100g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Category |\n|----------|-------------|--------|----------|\n| **Methionine** | Eggs | 510mg | Sulfur amino acid |\n| **Cysteine** | Eggs | 340mg | Sulfur amino acid |\n| **Glucosinolates** | Broccoli | 85mg | Sulfur compound |\n| **Glucosinolates** | Brussels sprouts | 102mg | Sulfur compound |\n| **Alliin/Allicin** | Garlic | 12mg | Sulfur compound |\n| **Sulfur compounds** | Onion | 45mg | Various |\n| **Total Sulfur Load** | — | **High** | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Excessive flatulence: 9/10, onset 2-4 hours\n- Sulfurous smell (rotten eggs): 8/10, diagnostic\n- Bloating: 7/10, onset 2-6 hours\n- Diarrhea: 5/10, occasionally\n- Fatigue: 6/10, afternoon\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nSulfur Metabolism Analysis:\n├── Sulfur amino acid load: 850mg (very high)\n├── Glucosinolate load: 187mg\n├── Hydrogen sulfide production: Likely elevated\n├── Pattern: 15/15 sulfur smell episodes = high sulfur foods\n├── SIBO breath test: Positive for hydrogen sulfide\n├── CBS gene variant: Possible (slow sulfur processing)\n└── Gut microbiome imbalance: Sulfur-reducing bacteria overgrown\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid healthy vegetables\"** → **\"Sulfur compound overload with H2S-SIBO\"**\n> \n> Treatment approach:\n> 1. Reduce sulfur intake temporarily (4-6 weeks)\n> 2. Treat underlying SIBO with appropriate protocol\n> 3. Support molybdenum status (needed for sulfite oxidase)\n> \n> Low-sulfur alternatives:\n> - Eggs → Limit to 1 or use egg whites only\n> - Cruciferous → Carrots, zucchini, lettuce, cucumber\n> - Garlic/onion → Garlic-infused oil (fructans, not allicin), green onion tops\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 9: Caffeine Metabolism (CYP1A2 Slow Metabolizer)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Coffee makes me anxious and I can't sleep even if I have it in the morning\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Coffee | 400ml (2 cups) | FDC |\n| Dark chocolate | 50g | FDC, FooDB |\n| Green tea | 250ml | FDC, MEXT |\n| Energy bar with guarana | 1 bar | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Half-life Impact |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------------------|\n| **Caffeine** | Coffee | 190mg | — |\n| **Caffeine** | Dark chocolate | 43mg | — |\n| **Caffeine** | Green tea | 35mg | — |\n| **Caffeine** | Energy bar | 80mg | — |\n| **Total Caffeine** | — | **348mg** | — |\n| **Theobromine** | Dark chocolate | 450mg | Related methylxanthine |\n| **L-theanine** | Green tea | 25mg | Mitigates anxiety |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Anxiety/jitteriness: 8/10, onset 30-60 min, lasts 8+ hours\n- Insomnia: 9/10, even with morning-only coffee\n- Heart palpitations: 6/10, intermittent\n- GI upset: 5/10, stomach acid\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nCaffeine Metabolism Analysis:\n├── Total caffeine: 348mg\n├── Timing: All consumed before 2pm\n├── Sleep onset: Still delayed by 2+ hours\n├── Normal half-life: 5-6 hours\n├── THIS USER's half-life: ~10-12 hours (slow metabolizer)\n├── Pattern: ANY caffeine after 8am affects sleep\n├── Genetic indicator: CYP1A2 slow metabolizer likely\n│   └── Morning coffee still circulating at midnight\n├── Theobromine: Adding to stimulant load\n└── Note: L-theanine in tea slightly reduces anxiety\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid coffee\"** → **\"Slow caffeine metabolizer—timing and dose critical\"**\n> \n> Personalized approach:\n> - Maximum caffeine: 100mg total per day\n> - Cutoff time: None after 6am (yes, 6am)\n> - Consider: Decaf coffee (still has 5-15mg)\n> - Alternative: L-theanine supplement for focus without jitters\n> - Hidden caffeine sources to track: Chocolate, tea, sodas, medications\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 10: Artificial Sweetener GI Distress (Sugar Alcohols)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Sugar-free products give me explosive diarrhea\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Sugar-free gum | 6 pieces | FDC |\n| Protein bar (sugar-free) | 1 bar (60g) | FDC |\n| Sugar-free ice cream | 150g | FDC |\n| Diet soda | 500ml | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Type |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------|\n| **Sorbitol** | Gum | 3.6g | Sugar alcohol |\n| **Maltitol** | Protein bar | 18g | Sugar alcohol |\n| **Erythritol** | Ice cream | 12g | Sugar alcohol |\n| **Aspartame** | Diet soda | 180mg | Artificial sweetener |\n| **Total Sugar Alcohols** | — | **33.6g** | Laxative threshold: 10-20g |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Diarrhea: 9/10, onset 2-4 hours, urgent\n- Bloating: 8/10, onset 1-2 hours\n- Cramping: 7/10, comes in waves\n- Gas: 8/10, excessive\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nSugar Alcohol Analysis:\n├── Total sugar alcohols: 33.6g (2-3x laxative threshold)\n├── Maltitol: Worst offender (only 50% absorbed)\n│   └── 18g maltitol = ~9g reaching colon = fermentation + osmotic diarrhea\n├── Sorbitol: Also poorly absorbed (contributes)\n├── Erythritol: Actually well-tolerated alone (90% absorbed)\n├── Pattern: 12/12 diarrhea episodes = sugar alcohols >15g\n├── Aspartame: NOT correlating with symptoms\n└── Cumulative effect: Multiple sources = disaster\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid all sugar-free products\"** → **\"Avoid sorbitol and maltitol specifically\"**\n> \n> Sugar alcohol hierarchy (best to worst tolerated):\n> 1. Erythritol - Usually fine up to 30g\n> 2. Xylitol - Moderate (threshold ~15g)\n> 3. Sorbitol - Problematic (threshold ~10g)\n> 4. Maltitol - Worst (causes issues at ~5g)\n> \n> Better sweeteners: Stevia, monk fruit, allulose (rare sugar, not sugar alcohol)\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 11: Lectin Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Beans and whole grains make me feel terrible—brain fog, fatigue, joint pain\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Kidney beans | 200g cooked | FDC, CNF |\n| Whole wheat pasta | 150g cooked | FDC |\n| Peanuts | 50g | FDC |\n| Tomatoes | 100g | FDC |\n| Quinoa | 100g cooked | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount/Concern | Type |\n|----------|-------------|----------------|------|\n| **PHA (Phytohaemagglutinin)** | Kidney beans | High | Lectin |\n| **WGA (Wheat germ agglutinin)** | Whole wheat | Moderate | Lectin |\n| **Peanut agglutinin** | Peanuts | Moderate | Lectin |\n| **Tomato lectin** | Tomatoes | Low-moderate | Lectin |\n| **Quinoa saponins** | Quinoa | Moderate | Related anti-nutrient |\n| Total lectin exposure | — | **Very High** | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Brain fog: 8/10, onset 2-4 hours, lasts 24+ hours\n- Fatigue: 8/10, profound, next day\n- Joint pain: 6/10, onset 6-12 hours\n- GI upset: 5/10, bloating and discomfort\n- Skin issues: 4/10, mild acne flare\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nLectin Pattern Analysis:\n├── Multiple high-lectin foods in single meal\n├── Kidney beans: Highest lectin (not fully deactivated)\n│   └── Canned? Dried and slow-cooked? Cooking method matters\n├── WGA: Even in cooked wheat, some remains\n├── Pattern: 10/12 brain fog episodes = lectin-heavy meals\n├── Autoimmune marker: Elevated zonulin (leaky gut indicator)\n├── Note: Properly prepared legumes cause fewer issues\n│   └── Pressure cooking reduces lectins 95%+\n│   └── Canned beans usually safe\n└── Gut barrier likely compromised\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid all beans and grains\"** → **\"Reduce lectin load and improve preparation\"**\n> \n> Preparation methods that reduce lectins:\n> - Beans: Soak 24 hours, pressure cook, or use canned\n> - Grains: Choose white rice over brown (less WGA in refined)\n> - Tomatoes: Peel and deseed, or cook thoroughly\n> - Peanuts: Roasting reduces (but doesn't eliminate)\n> \n> Consider 30-day lectin elimination, then reintroduce prepared properly\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 12: Phytate-Induced Mineral Deficiency\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I eat so healthy but I'm exhausted, hair falling out, always cold\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Oatmeal | 80g dry | FDC |\n| Whole grain bread | 100g | FDC |\n| Brown rice | 200g cooked | FDC |\n| Beans | 150g | FDC |\n| Nuts and seeds | 60g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Effect |\n|----------|-------------|--------|--------|\n| **Phytic acid** | Oatmeal | 840mg | Mineral binder |\n| **Phytic acid** | Whole grain bread | 600mg | Mineral binder |\n| **Phytic acid** | Brown rice | 580mg | Mineral binder |\n| **Phytic acid** | Beans | 720mg | Mineral binder |\n| **Phytic acid** | Nuts/seeds | 980mg | Mineral binder |\n| **Total Phytate** | — | **3,720mg** | — |\n| Iron (consumed) | — | 18mg | — |\n| Zinc (consumed) | — | 14mg | — |\n| Iron (absorbed estimate) | — | ~2mg | Very low |\n| Zinc (absorbed estimate) | — | ~3mg | Very low |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatigue: 8/10, constant, worsening over months\n- Hair loss: Noticed, diffuse thinning\n- Cold intolerance: 7/10, always freezing\n- Brittle nails: Noticed\n- Brain fog: 6/10, difficulty concentrating\n- Low mood: 6/10, recently developed\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nMineral Absorption Analysis:\n├── Phytate:iron ratio: 207:1 (should be <1:1 for absorption)\n├── Phytate:zinc ratio: 266:1 (should be <15:1)\n├── Lab correlation:\n│   ├── Ferritin: 12 ng/mL (low, explains fatigue/hair/cold)\n│   ├── Zinc: Low-normal\n│   └── B12: Normal (not the issue)\n├── Pattern: \"Healthy whole foods\" diet = severe phytate load\n├── Irony: High-fiber diet causing deficiency\n└── Vegetarian: Yes, compounding the issue (no heme iron)\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"your diet is bad\"** → **\"Phytate-induced iron and zinc malabsorption\"**\n> \n> Strategies to improve mineral absorption:\n> 1. **Add vitamin C** with meals (breaks phytate-mineral bonds)\n> 2. **Soak and sprout** grains, nuts, beans (reduces phytate 50-70%)\n> 3. **Ferment**: Sourdough bread has 50% less phytate than regular\n> 4. **Space out**: Don't eat all high-phytate foods in same meal\n> 5. **Consider**: Some white rice instead of all brown\n> 6. **Supplement**: Iron and zinc away from high-phytate meals\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 13: Glutamate/MSG Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Chinese food and chips give me headaches and weird tingling\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Chinese takeout (various) | 400g | FDC |\n| Doritos | 100g | FDC |\n| Parmesan cheese | 30g | FDC |\n| Tomato soup (canned) | 250ml | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Form |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------|\n| **Free glutamate** | Chinese food (MSG) | 600mg | Added MSG |\n| **Free glutamate** | Doritos | 380mg | Yeast extract |\n| **Free glutamate** | Parmesan | 450mg | Natural |\n| **Free glutamate** | Tomato soup | 280mg | Natural + added |\n| **Total Free Glutamate** | — | **1,710mg** | — |\n| Sodium | Combined | 3,200mg | Often co-occurs |\n| Aspartate | Combined | 200mg | Related excitatory |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Headache (pressure type): 7/10, onset 30-60 minutes\n- Facial tingling/flushing: 6/10, onset 20-30 minutes\n- Chest tightness: 5/10, uncomfortable\n- Thirst: 6/10, intense\n- Numbness in extremities: 4/10, occasional\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nGlutamate Analysis:\n├── Total free glutamate: 1,710mg (very high)\n├── Threshold appears to be: ~400mg causes symptoms\n├── Pattern: 11/14 headaches = glutamate >500mg\n├── Interesting: Parmesan alone (450mg) sometimes OK\n│   └── Suggests: Rapid absorption (empty stomach) worse\n│   └── With protein/fat: Better tolerated\n├── MSG vs natural glutamate: Same compound, same effect\n├── Hidden sources identified: \"Natural flavors,\" \"hydrolyzed protein,\" \"yeast extract\"\n└── Sodium load: May be synergistic (dehydration)\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid Chinese food\"** → **\"Limit free glutamate to <400mg, especially on empty stomach\"**\n> \n> Hidden glutamate sources (label names):\n> - Monosodium glutamate (MSG)\n> - Hydrolyzed vegetable/soy protein\n> - Autolyzed yeast, yeast extract\n> - \"Natural flavors\" (often contains)\n> - Soy sauce, fish sauce\n> - Tomato paste, tomato products\n> - Aged cheeses, cured meats\n> \n> Mitigation: Eat glutamate-rich foods with fat/protein, not on empty stomach\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 14: Copper-Zinc Imbalance\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I've been supplementing zinc for immunity but now I have weird symptoms\"**\n\n### Foods + Supplements Logged\n| Item | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Zinc supplement | 50mg | — |\n| Oysters (occasional) | 100g | FDC |\n| Beef | 200g | FDC |\n| Pumpkin seeds | 30g | FDC |\n| Dark chocolate | 50g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Source | Amount | RDA |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----|\n| **Zinc (total)** | All sources | 78mg | 11mg |\n| Zinc | Supplement | 50mg | — |\n| Zinc | Oysters | 16mg | — |\n| Zinc | Beef | 8mg | — |\n| Zinc | Pumpkin seeds | 2.5mg | — |\n| **Copper (total)** | Food only | 1.2mg | 0.9mg |\n| **Zinc:Copper Ratio** | — | **65:1** | Ideal: 8-15:1 |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatigue: 7/10, developed over 2 months\n- Anemia symptoms: Pale, short of breath\n- Neurological: Numbness, tingling in feet\n- Hair changes: Loss, color change\n- Immune issues: Frequent infections (ironic)\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nMineral Competition Analysis:\n├── Zinc intake: 78mg (7x RDA)\n├── Copper intake: 1.2mg (adequate normally)\n├── BUT: High zinc blocks copper absorption\n├── Zinc:Copper ratio: 65:1 (severely imbalanced)\n├── Timeline: Symptoms started 2 months after zinc supplementation\n├── Lab correlation:\n│   ├── Ceruloplasmin: Low\n│   ├── Serum copper: Low\n│   └── Neutropenia: Present (copper deficiency sign)\n├── Zinc-induced copper deficiency: CONFIRMED\n└── Irony: Zinc for immunity caused worse immunity\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"stop eating zinc foods\"** → **\"Supplement-induced copper deficiency\"**\n> \n> Action plan:\n> 1. **Stop zinc supplement immediately**\n> 2. Increase copper-rich foods: Liver, shellfish, dark chocolate, cashews\n> 3. May need copper supplementation short-term (under medical supervision)\n> 4. Target ratio: 8-15:1 zinc to copper\n> 5. If supplementing zinc long-term, add copper at 1:10 ratio\n> \n> Nutri insight: System flagged Zn:Cu ratio deviation weeks ago\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 15: Tannin Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"Tea and red wine make me nauseous and give me stomach pain\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Black tea | 500ml (3 cups) | FDC, Phenol-Explorer |\n| Red wine | 250ml | FDC, Phenol-Explorer |\n| Unripe banana | 1 medium | FDC |\n| Dark chocolate | 40g | FDC, FooDB |\n| Walnuts | 30g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Type |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------|\n| **Tannins (condensed)** | Black tea | 450mg | Polyphenol |\n| **Tannins (condensed)** | Red wine | 380mg | Polyphenol |\n| **Tannins** | Unripe banana | 200mg | Polyphenol |\n| **Tannins** | Dark chocolate | 150mg | Polyphenol |\n| **Tannins** | Walnuts | 180mg | Polyphenol |\n| **Total Tannins** | — | **1,360mg** | — |\n| Catechins | Tea | 120mg | Related polyphenol |\n| Resveratrol | Red wine | 2mg | Different polyphenol |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Nausea: 7/10, onset 15-30 minutes\n- Stomach pain: 6/10, cramping type\n- Metallic taste: 5/10, after tea\n- Constipation: 6/10, if high tannin days continue\n- Headache: 4/10, sometimes accompanies\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nTannin Pattern Analysis:\n├── Total tannins: 1,360mg (very high)\n├── Tannin + empty stomach: Symptoms 3x worse\n├── Pattern: 13/15 nausea episodes = tannins >500mg on empty stomach\n├── Mechanism: Tannins bind stomach proteins → irritation\n├── GI history: Gastritis/GERD (preexisting)\n│   └── Compromised stomach lining more sensitive\n├── Interesting: Same tannin amount WITH food = tolerated\n└── Iron absorption: Also being blocked (secondary issue)\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid tea and wine\"** → **\"Tannin sensitivity—timing and pairing matter\"**\n> \n> Strategies:\n> 1. **Never drink tea/wine on empty stomach**\n> 2. Add milk to tea (binds tannins, reduces irritation)\n> 3. Choose lower-tannin options:\n>    - Green tea over black tea (fewer tannins)\n>    - White wine over red wine\n>    - Ripe fruits over unripe\n> 4. Limit total tannins to <500mg when stomach is vulnerable\n> 5. Separate from iron-rich foods by 2 hours\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 16: Goitrogen Thyroid Impact\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I'm eating super healthy but my thyroid is getting worse\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Kale smoothie | 100g raw | FDC, CNF |\n| Broccoli | 200g | FDC |\n| Cabbage (coleslaw) | 150g raw | FDC |\n| Cauliflower | 150g | FDC |\n| Soy milk | 500ml | FDC |\n| Flaxseed | 30g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Mechanism |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-----------|\n| **Goitrin** | Kale | High | Blocks iodine uptake |\n| **Goitrin** | Broccoli | Moderate | Blocks iodine uptake |\n| **Goitrin** | Cabbage | High (raw) | Blocks iodine uptake |\n| **Goitrin** | Cauliflower | Moderate | Blocks iodine uptake |\n| **Genistein** | Soy milk | 45mg | Inhibits TPO enzyme |\n| **Isoflavones** | Soy milk | 80mg | Thyroid disruption |\n| **Thiocyanate precursors** | Flaxseed | Present | Anti-thyroid |\n| Iodine (diet) | — | 80mcg | Below RDA (150mcg) |\n| Selenium (diet) | — | 45mcg | Below optimal |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatigue worsening: 8/10, despite sleep\n- Weight gain: Despite \"healthy\" diet\n- Hair thinning: Accelerating\n- Cold intolerance: Severe\n- Constipation: 7/10, new symptom\n- Brain fog: 7/10, can't concentrate\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nThyroid Interference Analysis:\n├── Goitrogen load: EXTREMELY HIGH\n├── Raw cruciferous: 450g/day (goitrogens intact)\n├── Soy: 500ml/day (isoflavones high)\n├── Iodine intake: 80mcg (53% of RDA)\n├── Selenium intake: 45mcg (low, needed for T4→T3)\n├── Pre-existing condition: Hashimoto's thyroiditis\n├── Lab trend:\n│   ├── TSH: Rising (2.1 → 4.8 over 6 months)\n│   ├── Free T4: Dropping\n│   └── TPO antibodies: Elevated\n├── Pattern: Thyroid worsened as \"healthy diet\" intensified\n└── Triple threat: Low iodine + high goitrogens + soy isoflavones\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"your healthy diet is bad\"** → **\"Goitrogen overload with inadequate iodine\"**\n> \n> For someone with thyroid issues:\n> 1. **Cook cruciferous vegetables** (reduces goitrogens 70-80%)\n> 2. **Limit soy** to fermented forms (tempeh, miso)\n> 3. **Increase iodine**: Seaweed, iodized salt, fish\n> 4. **Add selenium**: Brazil nuts (1-2/day), seafood\n> 5. **Raw cruciferous limit**: <100g per day when thyroid is compromised\n> \n> Note: Cruciferous are fine for healthy thyroid + adequate iodine\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 17: B6 Toxicity from Supplements\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I started getting nerve pain and numbness, but my diet is great\"**\n\n### Foods + Supplements Logged\n| Item | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| B-complex supplement | 1 daily | — |\n| Energy drink (B-fortified) | 2 cans | FDC |\n| Protein bar (fortified) | 2 bars | FDC |\n| Chicken breast | 200g | FDC |\n| Banana | 2 medium | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Source | Amount | RDA |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----|\n| **Vitamin B6** | B-complex | 100mg | 1.3mg |\n| **Vitamin B6** | Energy drinks | 40mg | — |\n| **Vitamin B6** | Protein bars | 20mg | — |\n| **Vitamin B6** | Chicken | 1.2mg | — |\n| **Vitamin B6** | Banana | 0.8mg | — |\n| **Total Vitamin B6** | — | **162mg** | UL: 100mg |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Peripheral neuropathy: 8/10, hands and feet\n- Numbness and tingling: 7/10, progressive\n- Balance problems: 5/10, new symptom\n- Photosensitivity: 4/10, noticed\n- Difficulty walking: 5/10, developing\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nB6 Toxicity Analysis:\n├── Total B6: 162mg/day (125x RDA, 1.6x Upper Limit)\n├── Duration: ~4 months of this intake\n├── Sources:\n│   ├── Supplement: 100mg (already at UL!)\n│   ├── Fortified foods: 60mg MORE\n│   └── Regular food: ~2mg (negligible)\n├── Pattern: Neuropathy started 6 weeks after adding energy drinks\n├── Mechanism: B6 toxicity causes sensory nerve damage\n│   └── Typically reversible if caught early\n├── Lab: Serum B6 extremely elevated\n└── Irony: \"Energy\" supplements causing debilitating symptoms\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"nerve problem to investigate\"** → **\"Vitamin B6 toxicity from supplement stacking\"**\n> \n> Immediate action:\n> 1. **Stop all B6 supplements immediately**\n> 2. **Stop fortified energy drinks and bars**\n> 3. Recovery expected over 2-6 months\n> 4. If no improvement, see neurologist\n> \n> Nutri prevention: System now tracks cumulative B6 from all sources\n> \n> Watch for B6 in: Energy drinks, protein powders, fortified cereals, B-complex, multivitamins\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 18: Iron Overload (Hemochromatosis)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I'm always tired and my joints hurt, but my iron supplement doesn't help\"**\n\n### Foods + Supplements Logged\n| Item | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Iron supplement | 65mg | — |\n| Beef liver | 100g (weekly) | FDC |\n| Red meat | 300g | FDC |\n| Fortified cereal | 60g | FDC |\n| Cast iron cooking | Daily | — |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Source | Amount | Type |\n|----------|--------|--------|------|\n| **Iron** | Supplement | 65mg | Ferrous sulfate |\n| **Iron** | Beef liver | 6.5mg | Heme iron (high absorption) |\n| **Iron** | Red meat | 7.2mg | Heme iron |\n| **Iron** | Fortified cereal | 18mg | Added iron |\n| **Iron** | Cast iron leaching | ~5mg | Variable |\n| **Total Iron** | — | **~102mg** | RDA: 8mg (men), 18mg (women) |\n| Vitamin C (with iron) | — | 150mg | Enhances absorption |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatigue: 8/10, constant and worsening with supplements\n- Joint pain: 7/10, especially hands\n- Abdominal pain: 5/10, right upper quadrant\n- Bronze skin tint: Noticed by family\n- Erectile dysfunction: New symptom (male patient)\n- Brain fog: 7/10, persistent\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nIron Status Analysis:\n├── Dietary iron: ~102mg/day (6-12x RDA)\n├── Lab results:\n│   ├── Ferritin: 850 ng/mL (extremely elevated)\n│   ├── Transferrin saturation: 75% (very high)\n│   └── Liver enzymes: Elevated\n├── Pattern: Symptoms WORSEN with iron supplementation\n├── Genetic test: HFE mutation (hereditary hemochromatosis)\n├── Mechanism: Body cannot regulate iron → organ accumulation\n├── Joints: Iron deposits in synovium\n├── Liver: Iron overload causing damage\n└── Critical: Iron supplements making disease worse\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"you need more iron for fatigue\"** → **\"Undiagnosed hemochromatosis—iron is the problem\"**\n> \n> **URGENT action required:**\n> 1. **Stop all iron supplements immediately**\n> 2. See hematologist for therapeutic phlebotomy\n> 3. Avoid vitamin C with meals (enhances absorption)\n> 4. Limit heme iron (red meat, liver)\n> 5. Avoid cast iron cooking\n> 6. No fortified cereals\n> \n> Nutri insight: Correlating fatigue + joint pain + high-iron diet flagged for testing\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 19: Vitamin A Toxicity from \"Superfoods\"\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"My skin is peeling, I have headaches, and I feel nauseous constantly\"**\n\n### Foods + Supplements Logged\n| Item | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Beef liver | 150g (3x/week) | FDC |\n| Cod liver oil | 15ml daily | FDC |\n| Sweet potato | 200g daily | FDC |\n| Carrots | 200g daily | FDC |\n| Multivitamin with A | 1 daily | — |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Source | Amount | Form |\n|----------|--------|--------|------|\n| **Preformed Vitamin A** | Beef liver | 26,000 IU | Retinol (toxic form) |\n| **Preformed Vitamin A** | Cod liver oil | 13,500 IU | Retinol |\n| **Preformed Vitamin A** | Multivitamin | 3,000 IU | Retinol |\n| **Total Preformed A** | — | **42,500 IU** | UL: 10,000 IU |\n| Beta-carotene | Sweet potato | 18,000 IU | Provitamin (safe) |\n| Beta-carotene | Carrots | 17,000 IU | Provitamin (safe) |\n| Vitamin D | Cod liver oil | 1,350 IU | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Headache (intracranial pressure): 8/10, daily\n- Nausea: 7/10, constant\n- Skin peeling: 6/10, especially lips and hands\n- Hair loss: 6/10, noticeable\n- Joint pain: 5/10, new symptom\n- Blurred vision: 4/10, intermittent\n- Irritability: 6/10, mood changes\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nVitamin A Analysis:\n├── Preformed vitamin A: 42,500 IU/day (4.25x Upper Limit)\n├── Duration: ~3 months\n├── Critical distinction:\n│   ├── Retinol (liver, fish oil, supplements): TOXIC in excess\n│   └── Beta-carotene (vegetables): Self-regulating, NOT toxic\n├── Main culprits:\n│   ├── Liver: 26,000 IU per serving (260% UL in one meal!)\n│   └── Cod liver oil: 13,500 IU daily\n├── Pattern: Symptoms started 6 weeks into liver + cod liver oil routine\n├── Mechanism: Retinol accumulates in liver → systemic toxicity\n└── Interesting: Would need 100+ carrots/day to cause toxicity\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"stop eating vegetables\"** → **\"Preformed vitamin A toxicity from animal sources\"**\n> \n> Immediate changes:\n> 1. **Stop cod liver oil** (use regular fish oil for omega-3)\n> 2. **Limit liver** to 50g once weekly maximum\n> 3. **Check multivitamin** for retinol vs beta-carotene\n> 4. Sweet potato and carrots are FINE (beta-carotene is safe)\n> \n> Recovery: 2-4 weeks after stopping, symptoms improve\n> \n> Nutri tracking: Distinguishes preformed A from provitamin A carotenoids\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 20: Potassium-Sodium Imbalance (Hypertension)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"My blood pressure is high even though I don't add salt to food\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Deli turkey | 100g | FDC |\n| Bread | 4 slices | FDC |\n| Canned soup | 400ml | FDC |\n| Cheese | 60g | FDC |\n| Pickles | 50g | FDC |\n| Banana | 1 medium | FDC |\n| Broccoli | 100g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Target |\n|----------|-------------|--------|--------|\n| **Sodium** | Deli turkey | 900mg | — |\n| **Sodium** | Bread | 580mg | — |\n| **Sodium** | Canned soup | 1,800mg | — |\n| **Sodium** | Cheese | 350mg | — |\n| **Sodium** | Pickles | 780mg | — |\n| **Total Sodium** | — | **4,410mg** | <2,300mg |\n| **Potassium** | Banana | 420mg | — |\n| **Potassium** | Broccoli | 290mg | — |\n| **Total Potassium** | — | **710mg** | 4,700mg |\n| **Na:K Ratio** | — | **6.2:1** | Target: 1:2 |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Blood pressure: 155/95 mmHg (Stage 2 hypertension)\n- Headaches: 6/10, morning\n- Edema: 5/10, ankle swelling\n- Fatigue: 5/10, constant\n- Frequent urination: At night\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nElectrolyte Analysis:\n├── Sodium: 4,410mg (192% of limit)\n├── Potassium: 710mg (15% of target)\n├── Na:K ratio: 6.2:1 (should be 1:2 or better)\n├── Hidden sodium sources:\n│   ├── Canned soup: 1,800mg (78% of daily limit!)\n│   ├── Deli meat: 900mg (processed)\n│   └── \"No salt added\" ≠ low sodium\n├── BP correlation: Every 1,000mg sodium over limit = +5mmHg systolic\n├── Pattern: BP highest on processed food days\n├── Potassium deficit: Would need 6x more to balance\n└── Note: User doesn't add salt but eats high-sodium processed foods\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"stop using the salt shaker\"** → **\"Massive hidden sodium + potassium deficiency\"**\n> \n> Sodium reduction strategy:\n> - Canned soup → Homemade or \"low sodium\" versions\n> - Deli meat → Fresh cooked turkey/chicken\n> - Cheese → Use less, or choose Swiss (lower sodium)\n> - Bread → Choose lower-sodium brands\n> \n> Potassium increase (target: 4,700mg):\n> - Add: Potatoes (900mg), avocado (700mg), beans (600mg), spinach (560mg)\n> - Banana alone isn't enough (420mg is 9% of target)\n> \n> Goal ratio: 1:2 sodium to potassium\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 21: Choline Deficiency (Fatty Liver)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I have fatty liver but I don't drink and I eat 'clean'\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical vegan day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Oatmeal | 80g | FDC |\n| Almond milk | 400ml | FDC |\n| Salad with beans | 300g | FDC |\n| Quinoa bowl | 200g | FDC |\n| Fruit smoothie | 400ml | FDC |\n| Nuts and seeds | 50g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | RDA |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-----|\n| **Choline** | Oatmeal | 15mg | — |\n| **Choline** | Almond milk | 4mg | — |\n| **Choline** | Beans | 45mg | — |\n| **Choline** | Quinoa | 23mg | — |\n| **Choline** | Nuts | 15mg | — |\n| **Total Choline** | — | **102mg** | 425-550mg |\n| Betaine | Combined | 180mg | Can spare some choline |\n| B12 | Combined | 0mcg | Also deficient |\n| Folate | Combined | 420mcg | Adequate |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatty liver on ultrasound: NAFLD diagnosis\n- Fatigue: 7/10, despite good sleep\n- Brain fog: 6/10, memory issues\n- Muscle weakness: 5/10, noticed\n- Elevated liver enzymes: ALT/AST mildly high\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nCholine Analysis:\n├── Choline intake: 102mg (19-24% of requirement)\n├── Severe deficiency: <25% of RDA\n├── Mechanism: Choline needed to export fat from liver (VLDL synthesis)\n│   └── Without choline → fat accumulates → fatty liver\n├── Vegan diet factor: No eggs (top source: 147mg/egg)\n├── B12 status: Also deficient (methylation impaired)\n│   └── B12 deficiency worsens choline needs\n├── Pattern: Fatty liver developed after 2 years strict vegan\n├── Interesting: \"Clean eating\" can cause deficiency\n└── Genetic factor: PEMT variant (common in women) = higher needs\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"you need to lose weight\"** → **\"Choline deficiency causing fatty liver\"**\n> \n> For vegans/vegetarians—choline sources:\n> - Eggs (if not vegan): Best source, 147mg per egg\n> - Soy lecithin: Add to smoothies\n> - Tofu: 100mg per cup\n> - Shiitake mushrooms: 58mg per cup\n> - Consider: Choline supplement (bitartrate or CDP-choline)\n> \n> Also address:\n> - B12 supplementation (essential for vegans)\n> - Betaine-rich foods: Beets, spinach, quinoa (partial sparing)\n> \n> Nutri insight: Tracks choline and flags vegan patterns at risk\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 22: Magnesium Deficiency (Migraines and Cramps)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I keep getting migraines and leg cramps despite eating well\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Breakfast cereal | 60g | FDC |\n| Sandwich (white bread) | 2 slices | FDC |\n| Chicken | 150g | FDC |\n| White rice | 200g | FDC |\n| Salad | 100g | FDC |\n| Coffee | 600ml | FDC |\n| Alcohol | 2 drinks | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | RDA |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-----|\n| **Magnesium** | Cereal | 25mg | — |\n| **Magnesium** | Bread (white) | 12mg | — |\n| **Magnesium** | Chicken | 38mg | — |\n| **Magnesium** | White rice | 18mg | — |\n| **Magnesium** | Salad | 15mg | — |\n| **Total Magnesium** | — | **108mg** | 320-420mg |\n| Calcium | Combined | 280mg | Needs Mg balance |\n| Caffeine | Coffee | 285mg | Mg depleter |\n| Alcohol | 2 drinks | 28g | Mg depleter |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Migraines: 3-4 per month, severe\n- Leg cramps: Nightly, waking from sleep\n- Eye twitches: Frequent\n- Anxiety: 6/10, baseline elevation\n- Constipation: 5/10, chronic\n- PMS symptoms: Severe (female patient)\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nMagnesium Analysis:\n├── Intake: 108mg (26-34% of RDA)\n├── Depletors:\n│   ├── Coffee: 285mg caffeine → increases Mg excretion\n│   ├── Alcohol: Further Mg depletion\n│   └── Stress: Patient reports high (depletes Mg)\n├── Refined grains: White bread, white rice (Mg removed in processing)\n├── Pattern: Migraines correlate with lowest Mg days\n├── Cramp correlation: 100% of cramp nights = Mg <120mg\n├── Ca:Mg ratio: 2.6:1 (should be 2:1 or lower)\n│   └── High calcium without magnesium = worse symptoms\n└── Modern diet pattern: 50% of population Mg deficient\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"you have a migraine disorder\"** → **\"Magnesium deficiency + depletion factors\"**\n> \n> Increase magnesium (target 400mg+):\n> - Pumpkin seeds: 150mg per ounce\n> - Dark chocolate: 65mg per ounce\n> - Spinach: 157mg per cup cooked\n> - Almonds: 80mg per ounce\n> - Black beans: 120mg per cup\n> - Switch white grains → whole grains\n> \n> Reduce depletors:\n> - Limit coffee to 1-2 cups\n> - Moderate alcohol\n> \n> Consider: Magnesium glycinate supplement (best for neuro symptoms, 300-400mg at night)\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 23: Iodine Deficiency (Brain Fog & Fatigue)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I'm exhausted and can't think clearly, but all my tests are normal\"**\n\n### Foods Logged (typical day)\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Eggs | 2 | FDC |\n| Salad with olive oil | 200g | FDC |\n| Chicken breast | 200g | FDC |\n| Vegetables | 300g | FDC |\n| Fruit | 200g | FDC |\n| Himalayan pink salt | Used | — |\n| Almond milk | 400ml | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | RDA |\n|----------|-------------|--------|-----|\n| **Iodine** | Eggs | 48mcg | — |\n| **Iodine** | Vegetables | 10mcg | — |\n| **Iodine** | Chicken | 10mcg | — |\n| **Iodine** | Himalayan salt | 0mcg | NOT iodized |\n| **Iodine** | Almond milk | 0mcg | — |\n| **Total Iodine** | — | **68mcg** | 150mcg |\n| Selenium | Combined | 75mcg | Important cofactor |\n| Goitrogens | Cruciferous | Low | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Brain fog: 8/10, constant\n- Fatigue: 8/10, despite adequate sleep\n- Cold intolerance: 6/10, always cold\n- Dry skin: 5/10, noticed\n- Weight gain: Slow but steady\n- Slow heart rate: Noted at checkup\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nIodine Analysis:\n├── Intake: 68mcg (45% of RDA)\n├── Critical factor: Himalayan pink salt = NO iodine\n│   └── Switched from iodized salt 1 year ago\n├── No seafood in diet: Major iodine source missing\n├── No dairy: Would provide 50-100mcg/serving\n├── Almond milk: Unlike dairy, contains NO iodine\n├── Thyroid labs: \"Normal\" but TSH at upper limit (3.5)\n│   └── Subclinical hypothyroidism developing\n├── Pattern: Symptoms worsened after dietary changes\n│   └── \"Clean eating\" removed iodine sources\n└── UK/Europe: Iodine deficiency is re-emerging\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"you're just tired\"** → **\"Iodine deficiency from salt and dairy elimination\"**\n> \n> Iodine sources to add:\n> - Seaweed: Nori (16-43mcg/sheet), kelp (varies widely)\n> - Fish/shellfish: Cod (99mcg/3oz), shrimp (35mcg/3oz)\n> - Dairy: If tolerated, milk (56mcg/cup), yogurt (75mcg/cup)\n> - Eggs: Already eating (24mcg each)\n> - **Use iodized salt** (77mcg per 1/4 tsp)\n> \n> Or supplement: 150mcg potassium iodide\n> \n> Nutri insight: Flags \"fancy salt\" users for iodine tracking\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 24: Fiber Type Mismatch (IBS-D)\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I added fiber for gut health but my diarrhea is worse\"**\n\n### Foods Logged\n| Food | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Psyllium husk supplement | 10g | — |\n| Whole wheat bread | 100g | FDC |\n| Apples with skin | 2 medium | FDC |\n| Beans | 150g | FDC |\n| Oat bran | 30g | FDC |\n| Prunes | 50g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Food Source | Amount | Type |\n|----------|-------------|--------|------|\n| **Soluble fiber** | Psyllium | 7g | Soluble, viscous |\n| **Insoluble fiber** | Whole wheat | 8g | Insoluble |\n| **Soluble fiber** | Apples | 3g | Soluble (pectin) |\n| **Both types** | Beans | 8g | Mixed |\n| **Soluble fiber** | Oat bran | 4g | Soluble (beta-glucan) |\n| **Sorbitol** | Prunes | 12g | Sugar alcohol |\n| **Total Fiber** | — | **30g** | — |\n| Insoluble:Soluble | — | 2:1 | — |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Diarrhea: 9/10, urgent, multiple times daily\n- Cramping: 7/10, comes in waves\n- Bloating: 7/10, constant\n- Urgency: 8/10, can't wait\n- IBS-D diagnosis: Pre-existing\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nFiber Analysis for IBS-D:\n├── Total fiber: 30g (adequate amount)\n├── BUT: Wrong TYPE for IBS-D\n├── Insoluble fiber (wheat, skins): Speeds transit MORE\n│   └── Worsens diarrhea-predominant IBS\n├── Prunes: Sorbitol content = osmotic diarrhea\n│   └── 12g sorbitol = laxative effect\n├── Beans: Raffinose = fermentation + gas\n├── Pattern: Symptoms worse with insoluble fiber increase\n├── What helps IBS-D: SOLUBLE fiber (slows transit)\n│   └── Psyllium actually good, but overwhelmed by insoluble\n└── Classic mistake: \"More fiber\" without considering type\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"avoid fiber\"** → **\"Switch to soluble fiber, minimize insoluble\"**\n> \n> For IBS-D:\n> \n> INCREASE (soluble):\n> - Psyllium: Good, continue but start low (3g) and build up\n> - Oats: Whole oats better than bran\n> - Peeled/cooked fruits and vegetables\n> - White rice, rice noodles\n> \n> DECREASE (insoluble):\n> - Whole wheat → White bread or sourdough\n> - Apple skins → Peeled apples, applesauce\n> - Raw vegetables → Cooked\n> \n> ELIMINATE:\n> - Prunes (sorbitol)\n> - Beans (introduce slowly, small amounts)\n> \n> Target ratio: 2:1 soluble to insoluble\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 25: MTHFR + Folate Form Sensitivity\n\n### Surface Correlation\n**\"I'm taking folic acid but still have high homocysteine and feel terrible\"**\n\n### Foods + Supplements Logged\n| Item | Amount | Source DBs |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| Folic acid supplement | 800mcg | — |\n| Fortified cereal | 60g | FDC |\n| Fortified bread | 100g | FDC |\n| Spinach | 100g | FDC |\n| Lentils | 150g | FDC |\n\n### Compound Analysis\n| Compound | Source | Amount | Form |\n|----------|--------|--------|------|\n| **Folic acid** | Supplement | 800mcg | Synthetic (needs conversion) |\n| **Folic acid** | Fortified cereal | 400mcg | Synthetic |\n| **Folic acid** | Fortified bread | 200mcg | Synthetic |\n| **Total Folic Acid** | — | **1,400mcg** | Synthetic form |\n| **Natural folate** | Spinach | 194mcg | Food folate |\n| **Natural folate** | Lentils | 358mcg | Food folate |\n| **Total Natural Folate** | — | **552mcg** | Already active |\n| Vitamin B12 | Combined | 2.4mcg | Adequate |\n\n### Symptom Pattern\n- Fatigue: 8/10, constant\n- Brain fog: 7/10, difficulty concentrating\n- Depression: 7/10, treatment-resistant\n- Anxiety: 6/10, constant baseline\n- High homocysteine: 18 μmol/L (should be <10)\n- Previous pregnancy loss: History noted\n\n### Compound-Level Correlation Discovered\n```\nFolate Metabolism Analysis:\n├── Folic acid intake: 1,400mcg (synthetic)\n├── Natural folate: 552mcg (food-based)\n├── MTHFR status: Homozygous C677T (genetic test)\n│   └── 70% reduced ability to convert folic acid → methylfolate\n├── Problem: Taking tons of folic acid but can't USE it\n├── Unmetabolized folic acid (UMFA): Building up in blood\n│   └── May actually BLOCK natural folate receptors\n├── Homocysteine elevated: Because methylfolate is what lowers it\n├── Pattern: Symptoms WORSE since starting fortified foods\n│   └── Fortification added to \"healthy\" choices\n└── Methylation impaired: Affects neurotransmitters (depression, anxiety)\n```\n\n### Actionable Insight\n> **Not \"take more folic acid\"** → **\"Switch to methylfolate due to MTHFR mutation\"**\n> \n> Action plan:\n> 1. **Stop folic acid supplement** - replace with methylfolate (L-5-MTHF)\n> 2. **Avoid fortified foods** or choose non-fortified versions\n> 3. **Increase natural folate foods**: Leafy greens, legumes, citrus\n> 4. Add methylated B12 (methylcobalamin) - works synergistically\n> 5. Consider riboflavin (B2) - MTHFR enzyme needs it\n> \n> Typical dose for MTHFR: 400-800mcg methylfolate + 1000mcg methylcobalamin\n> \n> Nutri insight: Distinguishes folic acid from natural folate forms in tracking\n\n---\n\n## Summary: The Compound-Level Advantage\n\n| Scenario | Surface Diagnosis | Compound-Level Insight |\n|----------|------------------|------------------------|\n| 1 | \"Gluten sensitivity\" | Fructan (FODMAP) threshold exceeded |\n| 2 | \"Fish allergy\" | Histamine + DAO inhibitors (alcohol, sulfites) |\n| 3 | \"Healthy smoothies are bad\" | Oxalate overload from superfoods |\n| 4 | \"Fruit allergy\" | Salicylate sensitivity |\n| 5 | \"Italian food hurts joints\" | Nightshade glycoalkaloids |\n| 6 | \"Home cooking causes pain\" | Omega-6:3 ratio from seed oils |\n| 7 | \"Cheese gives headaches\" | Tyramine + MAOI interaction |\n| 8 | \"Eggs cause gas\" | Sulfur compounds + H2S-SIBO |\n| 9 | \"Coffee sensitivity\" | CYP1A2 slow metabolizer (genetics) |\n| 10 | \"Sugar-free = diarrhea\" | Maltitol/sorbitol, not all sweeteners |\n| 11 | \"Beans are bad\" | Lectins + compromised gut barrier |\n| 12 | \"Healthy diet = tired\" | Phytate blocking mineral absorption |\n| 13 | \"MSG sensitivity\" | Free glutamate from all sources |\n| 14 | \"Zinc isn't working\" | Zinc-induced copper deficiency |\n| 15 | \"Tea makes me sick\" | Tannin sensitivity on empty stomach |\n| 16 | \"Healthy eating worsens thyroid\" | Goitrogens + low iodine |\n| 17 | \"Nerve damage mystery\" | B6 toxicity from supplements |\n| 18 | \"Iron doesn't help fatigue\" | Hemochromatosis (iron overload) |\n| 19 | \"Supplements causing symptoms\" | Preformed vitamin A toxicity |\n| 20 | \"BP high, no salt added\" | Hidden sodium + low potassium |\n| 21 | \"Clean eating = fatty liver\" | Choline deficiency (vegan) |\n| 22 | \"Migraines and cramps\" | Magnesium deficiency + depletors |\n| 23 | \"Brain fog, normal tests\" | Iodine deficiency (fancy salt) |\n| 24 | \"Fiber makes IBS worse\" | Wrong fiber type for IBS-D |\n| 25 | \"Folic acid not working\" | MTHFR - needs methylfolate |\n\n---\n\n## Implementation in Nutri\n\n### Correlation Engine Capabilities\n\n```typescript\ninterface CompoundCorrelation {\n  // Temporal Analysis\n  compound_id: string;\n  total_consumed: number;      // From merged_nutrients\n  symptom_onset_hours: number;\n  symptom_severity: number;\n  correlation_strength: number; // -1 to 1\n  \n  // Threshold Detection\n  apparent_threshold: number;  // Amount that triggers symptoms\n  confidence: number;          // Based on data points\n  \n  // Compound Grouping\n  compound_group?: string;     // \"histamine\", \"FODMAP\", \"nightshade\"\n  group_total?: number;        // Sum of all compounds in group\n  \n  // Multi-factor Analysis\n  cofactors?: {               // Things that make it worse\n    compound: string;\n    effect: \"amplifies\" | \"blocks_detox\" | \"additive\";\n  }[];\n  \n  // Source Tracing\n  contributing_foods: {\n    food_id: string;\n    food_name: string;\n    compound_amount: number;\n    source_db: string;        // Which database\n    confidence: number;       // From 4-layer scoring\n  }[];\n}\n```\n\n### Alert Triggers\n\nNutri should flag:\n- Compound groups approaching user's discovered threshold\n- Dangerous interactions (tyramine + MAOI)\n- Cumulative toxicity (B6, vitamin A from multiple sources)\n- Mineral ratio imbalances (Zn:Cu, Na:K, Ca:Mg)\n- Deficiency patterns (vegan + low choline/B12/iodine)\n\n### The Power of 200+ Compounds\n\nThis analysis is only possible because Nutri tracks:\n- **Biogenic amines**: Histamine, tyramine, phenylethylamine\n- **FODMAPs**: Fructans, GOS, polyols, lactose, excess fructose\n- **Anti-nutrients**: Oxalates, phytates, lectins, goitrogens\n- **Fatty acid profiles**: Complete omega-3/6 breakdown\n- **Amino acids**: Individual tracking including sulfur AAs\n- **Phytochemicals**: Salicylates, polyphenols, tannins, glycoalkaloids\n- **Vitamins by form**: Folic acid vs folate, retinol vs carotenoids\n- **Minerals with ratios**: Not just amounts but relationships\n\nStandard calorie counters see: \"Pizza - 285 calories\"\nNutri sees: \"Wheat (gluten, fructans, WGA) + dairy (lactose, casein) + tomato (histamine, lectins) + aged cheese (tyramine, histamine) + processed meat (nitrates, sodium)\"\n\n**That's the compound-level difference.**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3254,"cache_read_input_tokens":8500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3254},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CXnMVFSJVioyizxDCjceu","type":"assistant","uuid":"52ad8841-cd96-4ba4-98d4-17b374b1d255","timestamp":"2026-02-04T04:00:17.020Z"}
